  "response": "Verified the top suspicions against the fixture source before triaging. Four of the reads changed the verdict.\n\n## Verdict: 0 block, 7 warn, 48 ignore\n\nNo finding in this report justifies blocking the merge. The three `high`-severity findings all carry `agent_risk` 0.30, and nothing in the report exceeds 0.46 — there is no finding where an agent editing this code is likely to break something it couldn't see.\n\n## Confirmed false positives — 17 findings (31% of the report)\n\n**`weak_test_signal` × 16 (crime_00012–00027, severity medium, agent_risk 0.27) — ignore.** The assertions exist; they're in extracted helpers.\n\n`tests/command.exitOverride.test.js:11` defines `expectCommanderError()`, which makes four `expect` calls. Every flagged test calls it — e.g. `command.exitOverride.test.js:84`. The file even declares this in machine-readable form at line 9:\n\n```js\n/* eslint jest/expect-expect: [\"error\", { \"assertFunctionNames\": [\"expect\", \"expectCommanderError\"] }] */\n```\n\n`tests/ts-imports.test.ts:9` is the same shape: `checkClass()` makes two `expect` calls, and all eight flagged tests are one-line calls to it. These aren't tests that prove nothing — they're tests that factored their assertions out, which is the behaviour the detector's own `fix_shape` (\"assert behaviour, not implementation\") would encourage.\n\n**`hardcoded_localhost` (crime_00044, severity 0.6, agent_risk 0.20) — ignore.** `lib/command.js:2152` is a comment, not code:\n\n```js\n} else if ((match = arg.match(/^(--inspect(-brk|-port)?)=([^:]+):(\\d+)$/)) !== null) {\n  // e.g. --inspect=localhost:1234\n```\n\nIt documents Node's own `--inspect` flag syntax next to the regex that parses it. There is no URL and nothing to lift into config.\n\n## Warn — 7 findings\n\n| Finding | Severity / agent_risk | Why warn |\n|---|---|---|\n| crime_00051, crime_00052 — duplicate `choices()` in `lib/argument.js:99` and `lib/option.js:176` | medium / **0.12** | The most merge-relevant finding in the report. Two production files carry byte-identical validation bodies; an agent fixing a `choices` bug in one will leave the other stale. Under-scored — see below. |\n| crime_00003 — `lib/command.js` 2179 lines | high / 0.30 | Real and 7.3× threshold. Warn, not block: it's the coherent `Command` class of a parser library, and splitting it is a refactor, not a merge gate. |\n| crime_00005 — `_executeSubCommand` 120L | high / 0.30 | Mixes path resolution, `execArgv` rewriting, and spawn. The section an agent edits is genuinely not the section it breaks. |\n| crime_00007 — `parseOptions` 115L | medium / 0.29 | Core argument-parsing loop; highest-consequence function in the package. |\n| crime_00031, crime_00032 — 6 broken zh-CN doc links | low / 0.24 | Genuinely broken (URL-encoded Chinese filenames absent from disk). Cheap to fix, and an agent following them gets nothing. |\n\n## Ignore — the significant ones\n\n- **crime_00001 `\"PORT\"` scatter (agent_risk 0.46 — the report's highest).** All five sites are `examples/` files demonstrating env-var-backed options. Centralising a literal across standalone, copy-pasteable examples is the wrong fix.\n- **crime_00002 / 00009 / 00048 boolean naming (`required`, `variadic`, `mandatory`, `hide`).** These are the published public API surface. Renaming them is a breaking change; `crime_00002` at agent_risk 0.45 is the report's second-highest score for advice that would break every consumer.\n- **crime_00004 `typings/index.d.ts` 891 lines.** A `.d.ts` is a flat declaration surface, not coupled logic. Line count doesn't mean the same thing here.\n- **crime_00010 sync I/O.** `fs.existsSync`/`realpathSync` resolving a subcommand path during CLI startup, immediately before a spawn. There's no concurrent load to block — the async rewrite adds risk and buys nothing.\n- **crime_00028 logic-in-comments.** Reads \"Note: the previous (sinon) test had custom code for SIGUSR1…\" with a link to Node docs. That's rationale, which is what comments are for. The detector's own 0.65 confidence is honest about it.\n- **crime_00034–00042 weak matchers.** `toBeTruthy()`/`toBeFalsy()` on `_hasHelpOption` — technically weak, but each test asserts a real before/after state transition. Correct catch, negligible value.\n- **Large test callbacks, CHANGELOG, Readmes** (00006, 00029, 00046, 00047, 00049, 00050, 00053–00055). Long test suites and append-only changelogs are the expected shape.\n\n## Calibration notes for `crimes` itself\n\nThis run has a ranking inversion worth recording:\n\n- **Scope skew.** 32/55 findings (58%) are in `tests/`, plus 5 in docs and 2 in `examples/` — 71% outside production source. Only 13 findings (24%) touch `lib/`.\n- **`agent_risk` isn't doing the ranking work it's supposed to.** The spread is 0.11–0.46; nothing clears 0.5. All three `high`-severity findings sit at 0.30, ranked *below* several mediums. The top-ranked finding is a literal in an examples file; the finding I'd most want an agent to see (duplicate `choices()` across two production files) sits at 0.12, third-lowest in the report.\n- **Two concrete fixes:** `weak_test_signal` should follow single-expression test bodies into a called helper — and when a file carries an eslint `jest/expect-expect` `assertFunctionNames` directive, that's an explicit, parseable declaration of which helpers assert. `hardcoded_localhost` is matching inside `//` comments.\n\nKilling the 17 false positives and the test/doc/example noise would leave roughly 7 findings, all in `lib/` — which is about the right size for a review of this diff.",
